WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.579 --> 00:00:05.960
In many programming languages, objects should
be closed when they are no longer needed to

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.090
save memory usage on a device from the app.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:14.889
In file management, files no longer needed
should be closed so that one does not accidentally

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:18.779
alter a file that should not be altered.

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:23.150
Closing a file frees up memory space the file
is taking while in use, thus helping a device

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:24.840
run more efficiently.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.529
Additionally, closing a file often allows
it to be safely modified by other programs.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:34.960
In the previous topic, we saw how to open
a file.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:41.370
We will continue going through the process
of working with a file used in game management.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:49.489
In this version of the file, 312-close, we
have, on line 6, opened the 312-message text

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:50.489
file.

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:54.840
We will eventually write some text to this
file, but I want to ensure that when we are

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.309
done with the file, we close it.

00:00:57.309 --> 00:01:08.670
Thus, on line 8, I will add this text: message_text.close().

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:16.619
The majority of programming languages use
parentheses for method and function calls

00:01:16.619 --> 00:01:18.120
and definitions.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.170
The parentheses serve multiple purposes.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:27.450
First, it makes functions easily identifiable
and maintains specific syntax.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:34.200
Second, functions often require arguments—the
values that get passed into the functions—and

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.590
parentheses serve as a container for those
arguments.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:44.210
It's worth noting that not all functions require
arguments, and there are some cases where

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:50.560
functions can be passed as arguments to other
functions without invoking them, without parentheses.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.760
Those exceptions, for example, are in a language
like Structured Query Language or SQL.

